\section{What "solved" means, and what it does not}{

Uniqueness is not proof. If the true assignment is excluded -- say more
items than \code{max_zeros} genuinely do not belong -- a coincidental one can fit
alone and be reported as certain. Read a \code{status} of \code{"unique"} as "only one
combination reaches the total", not as "this combination is right".

Nothing is dropped. Every input row comes back, and \code{status} says what
happened to its group:
\itemize{
\item \code{"unique"} -- exactly one assignment reaches the total.
\item \code{"ambiguous"} -- several do. Items that get the \emph{same} sign in all of them
are still determined, so their \code{sign} is filled in with \code{certain = TRUE};
the rest are \code{NA}. This is a logical consequence, not a guess.
\item \code{"unsolved"} -- no assignment reaches the total within \code{max_zeros}.
\item \code{"no_total"} / \code{"duplicate_total"} -- the group has no row labelled
\code{total_label}, or more than one.
}

Among competing assignments, those using the fewest zeros are preferred: a
solution that uses every reported line item is more plausible than one that
discards two. \code{status} and \code{n_solutions} describe the group \emph{after} that
preference is applied.
}

\section{Why a search, and not the taxonomy's weights}{

XBRL has a place for these weights: the calculation linkbase. The Danish
taxonomy does not fill it. Across its 13 versions from 2010 to 2025 the one
calculation linkbase is a 2020 income statement for one kind of education
institution, so for an ordinary annual report the arithmetic is the only
route to a sign. The taxonomy does mark every line debit or credit. Over
8,633 income statement lines solved in a 4,304-filing corpus and reported
positive, the sign found here agrees with that marking for 99.8\%, and the
rest are elements whose direction varies by nature, such as changes in
inventories.
}

